<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A graph is <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally irregular</em> if adjacent vertices have distinct
degrees. A graph <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decomposable</em> if its edge set can be decomposed
into locally irregular graphs, and its <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally irregular chromatic
index</em> <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the least number of graphs in such a decomposition. We
prove that <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 18</annotation></semantics></math> for every decomposable graph <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, improving the
previous bound of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="220" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>220</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">220</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>locally irregular graph, edge-decomposition, locally irregular
chromatic index
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 05C15; Secondary 05C70
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper, all graphs are finite and simple. A graph is
<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally irregular</em> if the endvertices of every edge have distinct
degrees.
An edge-colouring is locally irregular if every colour class forms a locally
irregular graph.
A graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decomposable</em> if it admits such a colouring, and <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
is the least number of colours in such a colouring.
The family of non-decomposable graphs was characterised by Baudon, Bensmail,
Przybyło, and Woźniak <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, who conjectured that every
decomposable graph <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, Sedlar and Škrekovski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> exhibited the <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bow-tie</em>,
obtained from an edge by attaching two triangles at each end, with all new
vertices distinct, and proved that its locally irregular chromatic index
is <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.
They later proposed the following conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected decomposable graph that is not the bow-tie, then
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Baudon, Bensmail, and Sopena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> proved that deciding whether a
given graph admits a locally irregular edge-colouring with at most two colours
is NP-complete, even for graphs of maximum degree at most <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, for trees they gave a linear-time algorithm that detects the
non-decomposable case and otherwise computes <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Przybyło <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> proved that every decomposable graph of sufficiently
large minimum degree satisfies <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Bensmail, Merker, and Thomassen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 328" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>328</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 328</annotation></semantics></math>
for any decomposable graph <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which was later reduced to <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="220" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>220</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">220</annotation></semantics></math> by Lužar,
Przybyło, and Soták <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
For results on particular classes of graphs, see, for
example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
Our main result improves the general upper bound from <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="220" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>220</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">220</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every decomposable graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 18</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A combination of Theorem 2.3 and Lemma 3.2 of Bensmail, Merker, and
Thomassen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> with Corollary 10 of Lužar, Przybyło, and
Soták <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> gives the following result, which may be of independent
interest.
For completeness, we show the proof in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. A general chromatic bound ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a decomposable connected graph, and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\equiv|E(G)|\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\equiv|E(G)|\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 4\chi(G)-2+\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 4\chi(G)-2+\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given a positive integer <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and a graph <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling</em> of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
is simply a map <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon V(G)\to\{1,\ldots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon V(G)\to\{1,\ldots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
An edge is <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">crossing</em> when its endpoints have distinct labels and
<em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monochromatic</em> otherwise.
The spanning subgraphs of <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of these two types of edges are the
<em id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">crossing graph</em> and the <em id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monochromatic graph</em>, respectively.
The labelling is <em id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">crossing-optimal</em> if it maximises the number of crossing
edges.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Overview of the proof</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since colours may be reused in distinct components, it is enough to consider a
connected graph <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fix a crossing-optimal <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be its
crossing and monochromatic graphs.
A switching argument shows that <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is connected (<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2. ‣ 4.2. Selecting crossing edges while preserving connectivity ‣ 4. Preserving connectivity ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
If <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, our <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>-colour bound for decomposable <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-colourable graphs
applies (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.2. The six-colourable case ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and group the six labels into three pairs
<math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},B_{2},B_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},B_{2},B_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subgraph of <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the vertices
with a label in <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; these three graphs edge-partition <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with an edge, orient it so that a subset of crossing edges can
later be added to make the union locally irregular, with the necessary
edge-count parity (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>).
Four crossing edges can then be assigned to each oriented monochromatic edge,
one to each label outside its pair, with all assigned edges distinct and such
that deleting them leaves <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> connected (<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 4.4. ‣ 4.2. Selecting crossing edges while preserving connectivity ‣ 4. Preserving connectivity ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).
For each <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, choose a subset <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of its assigned edges so that <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}\cup Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}\cup Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular.
Since <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(J_{i_{0}})\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(J_{i_{0}})\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and
whenever <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(J_{i})\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(J_{i})\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, assure that <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(Q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(Q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is even,
and that <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(Q_{i_{0}})\equiv e(C)\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(Q_{i_{0}})\equiv e(C)\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The unused crossing graph then has an even number of edges, it remains
connected, and it is <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-colourable, so it needs at most <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> further classes
(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.2. The six-colourable case ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).
The total number of colours is thus at most <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3+15=18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3+15=18</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Structural information by orientations</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Auxiliary multigraphs are finite and loopless, but may have parallel edges.
For a graph or auxiliary multigraph <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, put <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(H):=|E(H)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(H):=|E(H)|</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>
for the degree of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientation of <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{H}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{H}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for its indegree.
For a set <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> of vertices, write <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> for the number of edges from <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
to <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H[Y]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subgraph induced by <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an edge set <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-A</annotation></semantics></math> be the spanning subgraph with edge set
<math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(H)\setminus A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(H)\setminus A</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use the following elementary fact.
Its even-orientation special case is standard; see, for example, Cannon,
Ishaque, and Tóth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
We include a short proof for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected loopless multigraph.
Then</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every map </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\colon V(L)\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\colon V(L)\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and every spanning tree </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, every orientation of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-E(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-E(T)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> extends to an orientation </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{L}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfying </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(v)\equiv\eta(v)\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(v)\equiv\eta(v)\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and
only if </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V(L)}\eta(v)\equiv e(L)\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V(L)}\eta(v)\equiv e(L)\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an orientation of </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in which all indegrees are even,
except possibly one, which may have indegree </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Necessity in (i) follows from <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V(L)}d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(v)=e(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V(L)}d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(v)=e(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, suppose that <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V(L)}\eta(v)\equiv e(L)\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V(L)}\eta(v)\equiv e(L)\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, fix
an arbitrary orientation of <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-E(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-E(T)</annotation></semantics></math>, root <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and process the
non-root vertices with children preceding their parents.
At each non-root vertex, its parent edge is the only unoriented incident
edge; orient it to obtain the prescribed parity.
The resulting orientation <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{L}</annotation></semantics></math> also has the prescribed parity at <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>,
since</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(r)\equiv e(L)-\sum_{v\neq r}\eta(v)\equiv\eta(r)\pmod{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(r)\equiv e(L)-\sum_{v\neq r}\eta(v)\equiv\eta(r)\pmod{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (ii), the assertion is immediate if <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(L)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(L)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Otherwise, choose a leaf <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> of a spanning tree <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, orient all non-tree
edges at <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> away from <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and orient the other non-tree edges
arbitrarily.
Apply (i) with <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(\ell)\equiv e(L)\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(\ell)\equiv e(L)\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math> elsewhere,
and let <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be the resulting orientation.
Since only the tree edge at <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> can enter <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(\ell)\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{L}}(\ell)\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, while every other vertex has even indegree.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is due to Akbari, Dalirrooyfard, Ehsani, Ozeki, and
Sherkati <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Lemma 11 in the authors’ preprint]</cite>.
Reversing all orientations gives the indegree version below.
We include a short proof to make explicit that it also holds for multigraphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite loopless multigraph with finite forbidden sets of
non-negative integers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for each vertex <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> has an orientation <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D}(v)\geq|F(v)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D}(v)\geq|F(v)|</annotation></semantics></math> at every
vertex <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> has an orientation <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(v)\notin F(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(v)\notin F(v)</annotation></semantics></math>
at every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix an orientation <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D}(x)\geq|F(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D}(x)\geq|F(x)|</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(N)</annotation></semantics></math>, and proceed by induction on <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If no <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(x)</annotation></semantics></math> contains either <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, orient every edge from one
fixed bipartition class to the other.
Every indegree is then either <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so this orientation
avoids <math id="S2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Otherwise, choose <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in F(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in F(u)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(u)\in F(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(u)\in F(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D}(u)\geq|F(u)|\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D}(u)\geq|F(u)|\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, some edge <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vu</annotation></semantics></math> is directed from <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Put <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}:=N-vu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}:=N-vu</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the restriction of <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">First suppose that <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in F(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in F(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{\prime}(u):=\{j-1\colon j\in F(u)\setminus\{0\}\},\qquad F^{\prime}(x):=F(x)\quad(x\neq u)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}(u):=\{j-1\colon j\in F(u)\setminus\{0\}\},\qquad F^{\prime}(x):=F(x)\quad(x\neq u)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">We have <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(u)=d^{-}_{D}(u)-1\geq|F^{\prime}(u)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(u)=d^{-}_{D}(u)-1\geq|F^{\prime}(u)|</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(x)=d^{-}_{D}(x)\geq|F^{\prime}(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(x)=d^{-}_{D}(x)\geq|F^{\prime}(x)|</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq u</annotation></semantics></math>.
By induction, <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has an orientation <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(x)\notin F^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(x)\notin F^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Restore <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vu</annotation></semantics></math> directed from <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining an orientation <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(u)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(u)+1&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(u)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(u)+1&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(u)=j\in F(u)\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(u)=j\in F(u)\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(u)=j-1\in F^{\prime}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(u)=j-1\in F^{\prime}(u)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
For every <math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq u</annotation></semantics></math>, the restored edge is not directed into <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(x)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(x)\notin F^{\prime}(x)=F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(x)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(x)\notin F^{\prime}(x)=F(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> avoids <math id="S2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may therefore assume that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin F(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin F(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(u)\in F(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(u)\in F(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}(u):=F(u)\setminus\{d_{N}(u)\},\qquad F^{\prime}(v):=\{j-1\colon j\in F(v)\setminus\{0\}\}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}(u):=F(u)\setminus\{d_{N}(u)\},\qquad F^{\prime}(v):=\{j-1\colon j\in F(v)\setminus\{0\}\}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">and let <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}(x):=F(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}(x):=F(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin\{u,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin\{u,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Again, <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(u)=d^{-}_{D}(u)-1\geq|F^{\prime}(u)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(u)=d^{-}_{D}(u)-1\geq|F^{\prime}(u)|</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover, <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(v)=d^{-}_{D}(v)\geq|F(v)|\geq|F^{\prime}(v)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(v)=d^{-}_{D}(v)\geq|F(v)|\geq|F^{\prime}(v)|</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(x)=d^{-}_{D}(x)\geq|F(x)|=|F^{\prime}(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{D^{\prime}}(x)=d^{-}_{D}(x)\geq|F(x)|=|F^{\prime}(x)|</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\notin\{u,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\notin\{u,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By induction, <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has an orientation <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(x)\notin F^{\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(x)\notin F^{\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Restore <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vu</annotation></semantics></math> directed from <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, obtaining an orientation <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(u)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(u)\notin F^{\prime}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(u)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(u)\notin F^{\prime}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(u)\leq d_{N^{\prime}}(u)=d_{N}(u)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(u)\leq d_{N^{\prime}}(u)=d_{N}(u)-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(u)\notin F(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(u)\notin F(u)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(v)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(v)+1&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(v)=d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(v)+1&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; if <math id="S2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O}(v)=j\in F(v)\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O}(v)=j\in F(v)\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(v)=j-1\in F^{\prime}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{O^{\prime}}(v)=j-1\in F^{\prime}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
All other indegrees are unchanged, and hence <math id="S2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> avoids <math id="S2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By Hall’s theorem, the hypothesis of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> holds if and only if</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in S}|F(v)|\leq e_{N}(S)+|\partial_{N}(S)|\qquad\text{for every }S\subseteq V(N)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for every </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in S}|F(v)|\leq e_{N}(S)+|\partial_{N}(S)|\qquad\text{for every }S\subseteq V(N)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{N}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{N}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> counts edges with both ends in <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{N}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{N}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is the
set of edges with exactly one end in <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, match <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F(v)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F(v)|</annotation></semantics></math> copies of each vertex <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to distinct incident edges,
and direct those edges towards their assigned vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a bipartite loopless multigraph, and prescribe a common
bipartition direction on a matching <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of at most two edges.
Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be even, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
The prescription extends to an orientation whose indegrees are even or <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and in which no head of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has indegree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Call the heads of <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> marked and all other vertices ordinary.
Put <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=M-P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=M-P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Once the prescribed arcs of <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are restored, the total indegree of a marked
head is its indegree in <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> plus <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the total indegree of an
ordinary vertex is its indegree in <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Accordingly, for an ordinary vertex <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(v):=\{r\in\{3,4,\ldots,d_{N}(v)\}\colon r\text{ is odd }\}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is odd </mtext></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(v):=\{r\in\{3,4,\ldots,d_{N}(v)\}\colon r\text{ is odd }\}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">and, for a marked vertex <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F(v):=\{r\in\{0,1,\ldots,d_{N}(v)\}\colon r\text{ is positive and even,
or }r=k-1\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mtext> is positive and even,
or </mtext><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(v):=\{r\in\{0,1,\ldots,d_{N}(v)\}\colon r\text{ is positive and even,
or }r=k-1\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(v):=2|F(v)|-d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(v):=2|F(v)|-d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the prescribed edges have a common bipartition direction, all marked
heads lie in the same bipartition class.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">At an ordinary nonisolated vertex, <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd
and <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
At a marked head with <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(v)\geq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(v)\geq k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the value <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> when
<math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is even; at any other marked head it
is <math id="S2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
An isolated ordinary vertex has <math id="S2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">If (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) holds, the preceding Hall criterion and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>
give an orientation of <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> avoiding every <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
After the prescribed arcs of <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are restored, every total indegree is even
or <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and no marked head has total indegree <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may therefore assume that (<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) fails.
The difference <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in S}|F(v)|-e_{N}(S)-|\partial_{N}(S)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in S}|F(v)|-e_{N}(S)-|\partial_{N}(S)|</annotation></semantics></math> is additive
over the components of <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math>, so there is a set <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> is
connected and this difference is positive.
Since <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in S}d_{N}(v)=2e_{N}(S)+|\partial_{N}(S)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in S}d_{N}(v)=2e_{N}(S)+|\partial_{N}(S)|</annotation></semantics></math>, this is equivalent
to</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in S}\delta(v)-|\partial_{N}(S)|=2\left(\sum_{v\in S}|F(v)|-e_{N}(S)-|\partial_{N}(S)|\right)\geq 2\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in S}\delta(v)-|\partial_{N}(S)|=2\left(\sum_{v\in S}|F(v)|-e_{N}(S)-|\partial_{N}(S)|\right)\geq 2\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">A singleton cannot satisfy (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>): in that case
<math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\partial_{N}(S)|=d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\partial_{N}(S)|=d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, while the values above and the assumptions on <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
give <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(v)\leq d_{N}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(v)\leq d_{N}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If a connected set with at least two vertices contains at most one marked
head <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(v)\geq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(v)\geq k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, its <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-sum is at most <math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>: that
head contributes at most <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and, if present, has an ordinary neighbour
contributing at most <math id="S2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, while all other vertices contribute at most <math id="S2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains both marked heads, say <math id="S2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1},b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1},b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, each satisfying
<math id="S2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}(b_{i})\geq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}(b_{i})\geq k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of ordinary vertices in <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the two heads lie in the same bipartition class and <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> is
connected, <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each head contributes at most <math id="S2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-sum, and each ordinary
vertex contributes at most <math id="S2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) gives <math id="S2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq 4-q-|\partial_{N}(S)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq 4-q-|\partial_{N}(S)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus either <math id="S2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\partial_{N}(S)|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\partial_{N}(S)|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\partial_{N}(S)|=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\partial_{N}(S)|=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the latter case, <math id="S2.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the vertex set of a component of <math id="S2.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, both heads
have even degree in <math id="S2.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and both ordinary vertices have odd degree in <math id="S2.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique ordinary vertex in <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Direct every edge of <math id="S2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> towards <math id="S2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and direct the possible boundary edge
into <math id="S2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the resulting indegree of <math id="S2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, reverse one edge of <math id="S2.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math>
towards a head not incident with the boundary.
Such an edge exists because <math id="S2.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.
The indegree of <math id="S2.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is then even and, after the prescribed arcs of <math id="S2.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are
restored, each marked head has total indegree <math id="S2.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the underlying simple graph of <math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> has a perfect matching.
Direct every edge of <math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> towards the ordinary vertices and reverse the
direction of one edge corresponding to each edge of this matching.
Each ordinary vertex then has even indegree, since its degree in <math id="S2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
and each marked head has one incoming edge from <math id="S2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[S]</annotation></semantics></math> and one from <math id="S2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence total indegree <math id="S2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">There are no marked heads outside <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
The possible boundary edge was directed into <math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, so it contributes no
indegree outside <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Apply part (ii) of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> separately to the nontrivial
components of <math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N[V(N)\setminus S]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N[V(N)\setminus S]</annotation></semantics></math>, orienting their internal edges so that
every indegree is even or <math id="S2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Together, these orientations and those already fixed inside <math id="S2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, on the
possible boundary edge, and on <math id="S2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> give every vertex of <math id="S2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> indegree even
or <math id="S2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and give neither marked head indegree <math id="S2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">The next lemma shows how to combine a monochromatic subgraph with suitable
crossing edges to obtain a locally irregular graph, while prescribing the
parity of the number of crossing edges used.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with an edge and vertex set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
There exists an orientation <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{J}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> such that every bipartite graph
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(X,Y_{1}\mathbin{\dot{\cup}}Y_{2};E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∪</mo><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(X,Y_{1}\mathbin{\dot{\cup}}Y_{2};E)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{B}(v,Y_{i})=2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)\qquad\text{for every }v\in X,\ i\in\{1,2\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for every </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{B}(v,Y_{i})=2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)\qquad\text{for every }v\in X,\ i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contains, for each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a subgraph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(Q_{s})\equiv s\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(Q_{s})\equiv s\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\cup Q_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\cup Q_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Choose a vertex <math id="S2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of maximum degree <math id="S2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.
Among the orientations of <math id="S2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> in which every edge incident with <math id="S2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is
directed towards <math id="S2.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S2.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{J}</annotation></semantics></math> to maximise <math id="S2.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in X}b(v)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in X}b(v)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(v):=d_{J}(v)+2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(v):=d_{J}(v)+2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p19.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text">Reversing an arc <math id="S2.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> not incident with <math id="S2.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> changes this sum by
<math id="S2.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(b(u)-b(v)+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(b(u)-b(v)+2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence maximality gives <math id="S2.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(v)\geq b(u)+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(v)\geq b(u)+2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same holds on every arc <math id="S2.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(u)\leq 3d_{J}(u)-2\leq 3\Delta-2=b(x)-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(u)\leq 3d_{J}(u)-2\leq 3\Delta-2=b(x)-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the displayed degree condition.
For any integers <math id="S2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq q(v)\leq 2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq q(v)\leq 2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(v)</annotation></semantics></math> edges from
each <math id="S2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to each of <math id="S2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and pair them across the two sets.
Replace each pair <math id="S2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vy,vy^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vy,vy^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by an auxiliary edge <math id="S2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yy^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yy^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting multigraph <math id="S2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite and loopless.
Any orientation <math id="S2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{M}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> selects a subgraph <math id="S2.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subseteq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subseteq B</annotation></semantics></math> by
retaining, from each pair, the edge incident with its auxiliary head.
Thus</span></p>
