<div id="S2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">First put <math id="S2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(v):=2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)-\varepsilon(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(v):=2d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)-\varepsilon(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{J}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{J}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S2.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>
otherwise.
Apply part (ii) of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> separately to the nontrivial
components of <math id="S2.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of the resulting
orientations.
Let <math id="S2.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be the subgraph selected by <math id="S2.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In <math id="S2.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=J\cup Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=J\cup Q</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="S2.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in X</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S2.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(v)-\varepsilon(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(v)-\varepsilon(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, for every arc <math id="S2.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{J}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)-d_{H}(u)=b(v)-b(u)-\varepsilon(v)+\varepsilon(u)\geq 1\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)-d_{H}(u)=b(v)-b(u)-\varepsilon(v)+\varepsilon(u)\geq 1\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p21.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p21.2.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, every selected edge joins a vertex <math id="S2.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, for which <math id="S2.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and at least <math id="S2.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, to a vertex outside <math id="S2.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
whose degree is even or <math id="S2.p21.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.p21.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the other parity, change only <math id="S2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x)</annotation></semantics></math>: use <math id="S2.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is odd
and <math id="S2.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
The auxiliary edges arising at <math id="S2.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> form a matching <math id="S2.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is simple.
Prescribe them towards <math id="S2.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> with <math id="S2.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:=\Delta+q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:=\Delta+q(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is even and exceeds <math id="S2.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>; moreover, <math id="S2.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P|=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be the subgraph selected by the resulting orientation.
In the resulting graph <math id="S2.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=J\cup Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=J\cup Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(x)=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(x)=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every arc <math id="S2.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{J}</annotation></semantics></math> not incident with <math id="S2.p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(v)-d_{H}(u)=b(v)-b(u)-\varepsilon(v)+\varepsilon(u)\geq 1\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(v)-d_{H}(u)=b(v)-b(u)-\varepsilon(v)+\varepsilon(u)\geq 1\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">On an edge
<math id="S2.p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xu\in E(J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xu\in E(J)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.p22.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p22.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, whereas <math id="S2.p22.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is
even; if <math id="S2.p22.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{-}_{\vec{J}}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p22.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{H}(u)=d_{J}(u)\leq\Delta&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{H}(u)=d_{J}(u)\leq\Delta&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Every selected edge at <math id="S2.p22.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> ends at a head of <math id="S2.p22.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, whose degree is different
from <math id="S2.p22.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.
Finally, every selected edge not incident with <math id="S2.p22.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> joins an odd-degree vertex
of <math id="S2.p22.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> with degree at least <math id="S2.p22.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> to a vertex outside <math id="S2.p22.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of even degree or
degree <math id="S2.p22.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S2.p22.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">The original value of <math id="S2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> was <math id="S2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\Delta-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\Delta-1</annotation></semantics></math>
for even <math id="S2.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The change therefore reverses the parity of <math id="S2.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(Q)=\sum_{v\in X}q(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(Q)=\sum_{v\in X}q(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choosing the construction with edge-count parity <math id="S2.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> gives the subgraph <math id="S2.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>
for each <math id="S2.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Chromatic bounds</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>A general chromatic bound</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A bipartite graph is <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semi-even</em> if, in every nontrivial component, one
bipartition class consists entirely of even-degree vertices; this class may be
chosen independently in different components.
Such a graph is decomposable since it has an even total number of edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every semi-even forest has locally irregular chromatic index at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Lemma 3.2]</cite>.
Every semi-even bipartite graph has locally irregular chromatic index at
most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Corollary 10]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also use the following result of Bensmail, Merker, and
Thomassen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Theorem 2.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected decomposable graph.
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a locally irregular subgraph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> such
that each component of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-E(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-E(L)</annotation></semantics></math> contains an even number of edges.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next argument extends to multipartite graphs the semi-even decomposition
method used for bipartite graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
For a positive integer <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, write <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[k]:=\{1,\ldots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[k]:=\{1,\ldots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph in which every component has an even number of edges.
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> can be edge-decomposed into one possibly empty semi-even forest and
at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(H)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(H)-1</annotation></semantics></math> semi-even bipartite graphs.
Consequently,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(H)\leq 4\chi(H)-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(H)\leq 4\chi(H)-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:=\chi(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:=\chi(H)</annotation></semantics></math> and fix a partition <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\cup\cdots\cup V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\cup\cdots\cup V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)</annotation></semantics></math>
into independent sets. In every nontrivial component of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, choose a
spanning tree, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of these trees.
Orient every edge of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-E(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-E(T)</annotation></semantics></math> incident with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> away from <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and orient
the other edges of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H-E(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H-E(T)</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily.
Since every component has an even number of edges, part (i) of
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, applied separately in each nontrivial
component with even indegree prescribed at every vertex, extends the
orientation to all edges of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the spanning subgraph containing the edges
oriented towards <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. These graphs edge-partition <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
bipartite with even degrees in <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence semi-even.
Moreover, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}\subseteq T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}\subseteq T</annotation></semantics></math> by the choice of the non-tree orientations.
Thus <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. A general chromatic bound ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> gives
<math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(H)\leq 2+4(k-1)=4\chi(H)-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(H)\leq 2+4(k-1)=4\chi(H)-2</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.1. A general chromatic bound ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> gives a locally irregular subgraph <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
such that every component of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-E(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-E(L)</annotation></semantics></math> has an even number of edges; if
<math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be empty. Put <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}:=G-E(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}:=G-E(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. By
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1. A general chromatic bound ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
<math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq\tau+\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G^{\prime})\leq\tau+4\chi(G^{\prime})-2\leq 4\chi(G)-2+\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq\tau+\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G^{\prime})\leq\tau+4\chi(G^{\prime})-2\leq 4\chi(G)-2+\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> separately to the components and reusing
the same set of colours gives the best-known bounds of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> for decomposable
bipartite graphs and of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> for decomposable planar
graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
If every component has an even number of edges, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3.1. A general chromatic bound ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>
also matches the known bounds of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>The six-colourable case</h3>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>.
If every component of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has an even number of edges, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>.
Without the parity assumption, every decomposable graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">The result is trivial if <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is edgeless.
For the first assertion, it suffices to prove the bound separately for each
nontrivial component and reuse the same colours.
Thus we may assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and has at least one edge.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Identify the six available labels with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Among all proper maps <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\colon V(G)\to\mathbb{Z}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\colon V(G)\to\mathbb{Z}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, choose one
maximising the number of edges whose endpoint labels differ by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the spanning subgraphs <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U,T,O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U,T,O</annotation></semantics></math> (unit, two-step, and opposite) by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
