<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Proof of the main theorem</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since every component of a decomposable graph is decomposable and the same
set of colours may be reused in distinct components, we may assume that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
is connected.
Choose a crossing-optimal <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be its crossing and monochromatic graphs.
If <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is empty, then this labelling is proper, and the second assertion of
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.2. The six-colourable case ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> gives <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(G)\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Otherwise, partition <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[6]</annotation></semantics></math> into pairs <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},B_{2},B_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},B_{2},B_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and put
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}:=\varphi^{-1}(B_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}:=\varphi^{-1}(B_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}:=F[V_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}:=F[V_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[3]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The graphs <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1},J_{2},J_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1},J_{2},J_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> edge-partition <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with an edge, choose an orientation <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{J}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{J}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, and take the empty orientation otherwise.
Together these give an orientation <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
Apply <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 4.4. ‣ 4.2. Selecting crossing edges while preserving connectivity ‣ 4. Preserving connectivity ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, and let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the spanning subgraph of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>
consisting of the selected edges <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{a,\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{a,\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in E(\vec{J}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in E(\vec{J}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[6]\setminus B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[6]\setminus B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These graphs are edge-disjoint, and each arc contributes one edge to each
label outside <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is bipartite between <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and its complement, with</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{P_{i}}(x,V_{j})=2d^{-}_{\vec{J}_{i}}(x)\qquad\text{for every }x\in V_{i},\ j\in[3]\setminus\{i\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for every </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{P_{i}}(x,V_{j})=2d^{-}_{\vec{J}_{i}}(x)\qquad\text{for every }x\in V_{i},\ j\in[3]\setminus\{i\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">For every <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with an edge, let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1},Y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1},Y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the two sets in
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{j}\colon j\in[3]\setminus\{i\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{j}\colon j\in[3]\setminus\{i\}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and apply <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2. Structural information by orientations ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>
to <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For either prescribed parity, this gives a subgraph <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}\subseteq P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}\subseteq P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for
which <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}\cup Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}\cup Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally irregular.
If <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is edgeless, take <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> empty.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\in[3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\in[3]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> has an edge.
For each <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has an edge, choose <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with an even
number of edges.
Then choose <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(Q_{i_{0}})\equiv e(C)\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(Q_{i_{0}})\equiv e(C)\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\in[3]}e(Q_{i})\equiv e(C)\pmod{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\in[3]}e(Q_{i})\equiv e(C)\pmod{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}:=J_{i}\cup Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}:=J_{i}\cup Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[3]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:=C-\bigcup_{i\in[3]}E(Q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:=C-\bigcup_{i\in[3]}E(Q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The displayed congruence shows that <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> has an even number of edges and
contains the connected spanning graph <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C-\bigcup_{i\in[3]}E(P_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C-\bigcup_{i\in[3]}E(P_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which
is connected by <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 4.4. ‣ 4.2. Selecting crossing edges while preserving connectivity ‣ 4. Preserving connectivity ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.
Thus <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The graphs <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(J_{i})\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(J_{i})\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, together with <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>,
edge-decompose <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first assertion of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.2. The six-colourable case ‣ 3. Chromatic bounds ‣ An 18-colour bound for locally irregular decompositions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> gives <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(R)\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>irr</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{\prime}_{\mathrm{irr}}(R)\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>, and
therefore</span></p>
